9.	List the ID and the name of the division that has more than one employee whose salary is greater than the divisional average salary.  (hint: correlated subquery, group by, having). Also show the count of such employees of each division and divisional average salary by the side of division name (Hint: use subquery at outer Select clause) .  Note, if you do not use DID in group by clause, you may encouter error - think why.

Select d.did, d.dname, 
       (Select round(AVG(e.salary)) from Employee e where e.did = d.did) AS divisional_avg_salary,
       COUNT(e.empid) AS num_employees_above_avg
from division d, employee e 
where e.DID = d.DID and e.salary > (SELECT AVG(e1.salary) FROM Employee e1 WHERE e1.DID = d.DID)
GROUP BY d.DID, d.dname
HAVING COUNT(e.empID) > 1

If we do not use group by we encounter error because group by ensures that we are aggregating the results by each division. Without grouping by d.did, the SQL wouldn't know how to aggregate the data for each division

